# Freeze the character tools/keys/spook-mount.keys builds, so every check that
# runs that script can load him instead of rolling him again.
#
#   INCURSION_OPTIONS=tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat \
#       tools/headless.sh tools/keys/spook-mount-save.keys 5
#
# THE CHARGEN BELOW IS A COPY of tools/keys/spook-mount.keys lines 1-70,
# deliberately unchanged, and the original is NOT edited. That script builds its
# character inline rather than including a shared chargen-*.keys, so there is
# nothing to @include. Copying rather than refactoring means no existing check
# changes what it measures. The same choice, and the same warning, are in
# tools/keys/lizardfolk-monk-save.keys: do not "improve" this copy -- the
# fixture is only worth having if it holds THE character that script rolls.
#
# WHY A CHARACTER IS FROZEN AT ALL: see the header of
# tools/keys/chargen-default-save.keys. bd inc-vrt8.

# Does a mount keep its own aura when you climb on it?
#
# bd inc-izzy item 2. Spook is a mobile field centred on its caster
# (lib/wspells.irh:984-992). A night hunter that a player has tamed stands on
# the map and holds its aura. Creature::Mount (src/Skills.cpp:4300) takes the
# steed off the map with Remove(false) before it sets the MOUNT stati, and
# Thing::Remove destroys every mobile field created by the thing it removes
# (src/Display.cpp:2043-2053). So climbing on kills the aura the animal is
# still emitting from underneath you.
#
# A second defect lives at the other end of the same journey. Thing::PlaceAt
# carries a mount's fields to the new level but re-creates them under the
# RIDER's name, so the aura changes hands at every staircase.
#
# THE ORACLES are the character sheet's Hit line, which prints the morale
# term, and the dismiss menu ('x'), which lists a field only when the player
# is its recorded creator (src/Skills.cpp:398). Three states, in order:
#
#   1 tamed bat beside the player, on foot   Hit 4, "Spook" on the status line
#   2 the same bat, now ridden               Hit 7 and no Spook before the fix,
#                                            Hit 5 and Spook after it
#   3 dismounted, stepped out of the radius  Hit 6 and no Spook
#     and back in
#   4 mounted again after that               Hit 7 and no Spook
#   5 one staircase later, still ridden      the dismiss menu offers "Drop
#                                            Spook" before the fix and only
#                                            "Dismount" after it
#
# READ STATES 3 AND 4 FIRST. They are the ones that describe play. A night
# hunter you own must never demoralize you, on foot or in the saddle, and
# these two states are what says so.
#
# The -2 in states 1 and 2 is an artefact of how this run gets its bat. Wizard
# mode summons it HOSTILE, it spooks the player, and Make Player Master of
# Monster then tames it -- and taming does not scrub a penalty already taken.
# So the player walks into state 1 carrying a debt from before the bat was
# his. That debt is the only witness available to the aura's survival across
# the climb: he is immune on entry, so an aura that is alive and an aura that
# is gone look identical on his sheet unless he was already carrying one.
# States 3 and 4 clear the debt by stepping out and back in, and they read 6
# and 7 -- no penalty either way.
#
# State 1 is also the control. It must keep its -2 after any fix, or the "fix"
# merely stopped the bat casting. The "Dismount" row is the control for state
# 5: it proves the menu drew at all. State 5 reads the menu rather than a
# number because by then the player is immune and his sheet says nothing.
#
# Freeze Monsters is on so the bat neither attacks nor wanders off. It still
# raises its aura, because Monster::Initialize runs PreBuff at creation.

a       # Initial Choices: [a] Create a New Character
n       # Alter the character generation options? -- no
y       # Explore Mode -- yes
i       # Race:  [i] Orc
a       # Subrace: [a] Orc (Standard)
j       # Class: [j] Warrior -- a class with Ride and no spell menus
a       # Attributes: the first offered set
c       # Alignment: [c] Neutral
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Focus on which Weapon" a
@while "|  *" RIGHT RIGHT RIGHT DOWN
ENTER y
@while "wish to worship" y
@while "Choose a Domain" a
@while "|  *" RIGHT RIGHT RIGHT DOWN
ENTER y
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Focus on which Weapon" a
@while "|  *" RIGHT RIGHT RIGHT DOWN
ENTER y

@dump:arrival

# HOW MANY KEYS THE OPENING WANTS IS A PROPERTY OF THE CHARACTER, so no fixed
# count is right for every chargen. Measured 2026-09-17: tools/keys/chargen.keys
# leaves NOTHING pending and reaches the System Menu on the first ESC, while
# tools/keys/chargen-rogue.keys leaves a god's speech and an AutoHide line, so
# the first ESC only dismisses a message. A hard-coded ESC ENTER SPACE, copied
# from tools/keys/lizardfolk-monk-save.keys, failed in both directions: it
# walked INTO the menu for the barbarian and fell short of it for the rogue.
# @until sends ESC as many times as this character needs and stops the moment
# the menu is up (src/Wposix.cpp:1318-1326).
# CHARGEN IS NOT OVER UNTIL THE ARCHETYPE IS PICKED, and ESC will not dismiss
# that screen -- it is a menu wanting a letter. Measured 2026-09-17: five of
# these save scripts reached "Your personality archetype has little direct
# influence..." and spent all 200 of @until's passes pressing ESC at it
# (src/Wposix.cpp:1568-1571). [a] is The Ambitious Conqueror; the choice only
# has to be the SAME one every time, because a fixture is a frozen character
# and the archetype is, in the game's own words, "largely a 'colour' choice".
@while "personality archetype" a

@until "System Menu" ESC

# @until only warns when it gives up (src/Wposix.cpp:1568-1571), so the
# assertion stays: it stops the run at exit 6 rather than pressing a letter
# into whatever screen is really there. [a] beside [b] is Save and Quit.
@expect "System Menu"
b                       # [b] Save and Continue (src/Player.cpp:843)

# The save is not written by the keypress. Case 2 of the System Menu sets the
# autosave flag and Timeout = 1 (src/Player.cpp:851-853); Game::Play writes the
# file at the top of the next pass of its loop (src/Main.cpp:266-275). This
# dump therefore fires AFTER the file is on disk, and photographs the engine's
# own "Autosave... done." line saying so.
@dump:saved

# NO @quit HERE, DELIBERATELY. @quit becomes KY_CMD_QUICK_QUIT
# (src/Wposix.cpp:1657-1664), which is Save and Quit: it would write the save a
# SECOND time and shuffle the first copy off to <name>.sav.backup, leaving the
# fixture a file no line of this script asked for. Letting the key script run
# out instead ends the session with exactly one save.
